WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.302
One thousand shoppers open the same product page.

2
00:00:03.302 --> 00:00:06.838
Redis is fast, until its hottest cache key expires.

3
00:00:06.838 --> 00:00:09.194
Suddenly, every request wants the database.

4
00:00:09.714 --> 00:00:14.197
At noon, the product cache misses across multiple
Spring Boot replicas.

5
00:00:14.197 --> 00:00:17.864
Without coordination, each request starts the same
PostgreSQL query.

6
00:00:17.864 --> 00:00:19.902
This is a cache stampede.

7
00:00:20.422 --> 00:00:27.822
At one thousand requests per second and three hundred
milliseconds per query, roughly three hundred
overlapping reads can build up.

8
00:00:27.822 --> 00:00:30.412
That's a conceptual estimate, not a benchmark.

9
00:00:30.932 --> 00:00:33.970
First, merge duplicate loads inside each application
instance.

10
00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:40.047
This reduces pressure, but twelve replicas can still
start twelve database reads for the same key.

11
00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:42.208
Next, coordinate across replicas.

12
00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:45.080
A short Redis lease chooses one leader.

13
00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:49.593
That leader double-checks the cache, queries
PostgreSQL, and publishes the result.

14
00:00:49.593 --> 00:00:51.645
Followers re-read with a deadline.

15
00:00:52.165 --> 00:00:53.826
Never blindly delete a lock.

16
00:00:53.826 --> 00:00:56.817
If a lease expires, another leader may own it.

17
00:00:56.817 --> 00:01:00.140
Release only when the stored random token matches your
token.

18
00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:02.134
A lease is not exactly-once execution.

19
00:01:02.654 --> 00:01:05.303
For descriptions, stale-while-revalidate may be
acceptable.

20
00:01:05.303 --> 00:01:08.834
Add TTL jitter to spread expiration across keys.

21
00:01:08.834 --> 00:01:13.690
But payment totals and inventory must be validated
against authoritative data.

22
00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:17.148
The lesson: don't just add a cache.

23
00:01:17.148 --> 00:01:19.666
Design what happens when it misses.

24
00:01:19.666 --> 00:01:24.702
Coordinate expensive refreshes, bound waiting, monitor
contention, and keep checkout correctness separate.

